A1. If this "jack" isn't nimble, if this "jack" isn't quick, hard times will not be over, and depression is going to stick. ~ CAPIIAL
A8. A prize fight. ~ BRIDGE
A14. This one spends most of the time in South America and is continually taking a run down to the Atlantic coast. ~ ORINOCO
A15. These will go over big this season. ~ LINERS
A16. A tenant in lieu of something (abbr.). ~ LT
A17. What the lawyer took from his suit case. ~ FEE
A18. What liquor does from a wet plank. ~ TRICKLES
A20. These are always on time. ~ DIALS
A22. What Sandy thinks Mickey is. ~ MOOSE
A23. Here is a chance to browse in a poetic field. ~ LEA
A24. The way a negro turns when he sees a ghost. ~ ECRU
A25. This is where Will Rogers met his wife. ~ DEPOT
A26. Where Burgoo was a winner (abbr.). ~ KY
A27. In order to get into this you will have to show your berth certificate. ~ SLEEPER
A29. What 2 pounds of meat will weigh in India. ~ SER
A31. A beast of burden. ~ ASS
A32. You will need a topographical engineer for this (abbr.). ~ TE
A33. A popular figure among baseball players. ~ NINE
A34. What a city man misses most when he moves to the suburb. ~ TRAIN
A36. Here is where you will have to take the word for the deed. ~ ACT
A37. A spot remover. ~ TNT
A38. The part of Turkey that tourists do not care for (plur.). ~ NECKS
A41. Sport shoes. ~ SKIS
A43. As long as this is a short word you can make it accordingly. ~ SO
A45. The kind of mouth Chrysler has. ~ PLY
A47. The kind of cup that comes with a stew. ~ HIC
A48. Sanctimoniously. ~ PIOUSLY
A50. This has recently introduced a new field into Congress (abbr.). ~ IA
A51. What Congress almost did to the bears. ~ TREED
A53. Some very deer relatives that live in Europe. ~ ROES
A54. You all know Ra with the "sunny disposish," well meet Mrs. Ra. ~ MUT
A56. You can not do this in a horizontal position, so turn the puzzle around. ~ STAND
A57. Some one will surely make ridicule of this. ~ TAUNT
A58. The president of the United States has the largest collection of these. ~ PROBLEMS
A60. Although this carries a lot of avoirdupois, it is not backward. ~ TON
A61. At last we have found what started the depression. ~ DE
A62. Bonus heirs. ~ LEGION
A63. This has some class. ~ TEACHER
A65. Lifts the depression and makes one feel like a million dollars. ~ ELATES
A66. Captures the heart. ~ ENDEARS

D1. This is not so hot. ~ COLDEST
D2. A little item of interest we found in the paper. ~ ARTICLE
D3. This little two letter word may include every letter in the English alphabet. ~ PI
D4. Something you think you have until you start to use it. ~ INFLUENCE
D5. These extend a little over a foot. ~ IOES
D6. This looks good in any kind of suit. ~ ACE
D7. We did not have space to make this longer but consider the poor Indian. ~ LO
D8. Lots of bright sunshine will raise this. ~ BLISTER
D9. Young married couples in Scotland always have this for their first meal. ~ RICE
D10. "The colored slave that waits upon thought." ~ INK
D11. Even in the hills and the mountains you will find this depression. ~ DELL
D12. These have a monopoly on "All American restaurants." ~ GREEKS
D13. Literature for the drys. ~ ESSAYS
D18. We wonder if anyone is sitting on this part of the world now. ~ TOP
D19. Fowl sleeping quarters. ~ ROOST
D21. This has certainly been a backward era for many. ~ ARE
D22. Nothing but. ~ MERE
D25. A cubby hole. ~ DEN
D28. What they will do with the horseshoes in the future. ~ PITCH
D30. Two to one. ~ RATIO
D31. This one has always been very busy and yet this summer he was seen in a bread line. ~ ANT
D35. This has been the cause of many fires. ~ INSURANCE
D36. We are not particular about this. ~ ANY
D39. These have to get a licking every time they take a bath. ~ KITTENS
D40. Beat it. ~ SCRAM
D41. Accelerated. ~ SPED
D42. Fresh country butter. ~ KID
D43. These folks are very narrow. ~ SLENDER
D44. Something should be done about this. These were dragged from their beds, put on the pan and grilled and grilled. ~ OYSTERS
D45. Those who are learning to cook should never let this come to a boil. ~ PIMPLE
D46. This was a winner for the ancient Greeks. ~ LAUREL
D49. A little bit in Paris. ~ SOU
D52. A cockney will tell you that these never crow. ~ ENS
D55. This was always hanging around Caesar and his friends. ~ TOGA
D56. Whoever gets this plum will be lucky. ~ SLOE
D57. And it lived hoppily ever after. ~ TOAD
D59. We hope this will not bore you. ~ BIT
D60. What is Woolworth now? ~ TEN
D63. Aw! go tell urium (abbr.). ~ TE
D64. If you stutter with this you might get hysterical. ~ HA


